==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/fæd͡ʒ/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-fadge.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|ædʒ|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
{{unk|en}}.  According to Chambers, from {{der|en|ang|fēġan||to join or fit together}}; Liberman suggests a {{der|en|enm|-}} variant of {{m|en|fagot|t=bundle of sticks}}. Compare also {{der|en|ang|feċġan|t=to seize, take hold, bring to}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete|intransitive}} To be [[suitable]] ({{m|en|with}} or {{m|en|to}} something).
#* {{RQ:Wycherley Country Wife|act=IV|scene=iii|page=45|passage=Well, Sir, how '''fadges''' the new deſign; have you not the luck of all your Brother Projectors, to deceive only your ſelf at laſt?}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|intransitive}} To [[agree]], to get along ({{m|en|with}}).
#* {{RQ:Milton Divorce|passage=They shall be made, spight of antipathy, to '''fadge''' together.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|intransitive}} To get on well; to [[cope]], to [[thrive]].
#* {{RQ:Montaigne Florio Essayes|chapter=17|book=II|passage=I can never '''fadge''' well: for I am at such a stay, that except for health and life, there is nothing I will take the paines to fret my selfe about, or will purchase at so high a rate as to trouble my wits for it, or be constrained thereunto.}}
# {{lb|en|Geordie}} To [[eat]] together.
# {{lb|en|Yorkshire|of a horse}} To move with a [[gait]] between a [[jog]] and a [[trot]].

===Etymology 2===
{{unc|en}}, but potentially from or related to Old English {{m|ang|faċġ||flat-fish, plaice, flounder}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|Ireland}} Irish [[potato bread]]; a flat [[farl]], griddle-baked, often served fried.
# {{lb|en|New Zealand}} A wool pack, traditionally made of [[jute]], now often synthetic.
# {{lb|en|Geordie}} A small loaf or bun made with left-over dough.
# {{lb|en|Yorkshire}} A [[gait]] of horses between a [[jog]] and a [[trot]].

